\n    28\t    ;; descriptor rules, exactly as any other map's would.\n    29\t    [yang.call/_descriptor\n    30\t     (fn [callable]\n    31\t       (if (data/map? callable)\n    32\t         (if (if (= (get callable :type) :cell-ref)\n    33\t               (cell/authentic? callable)\n    34\t               false)\n    35\t           (cell/get callable)\n    36\t           callable)\n    37\t         nil))]\n    38\t    [yang.call/entry\n    39\t     (fn [callable]\n    40\t       (get (yang.call/_descriptor callable) :yang.call/entry))]\n    41\t    [yang.call/eligible?\n    42\t     (fn [callable]\n    43\t       (let [descriptor (yang.call/_descriptor callable)]\n    44\t         (if (nil? (yang.call/entry callable))\n    45\t           false\n    46\t           (if (data/contains? descriptor :yang.call/eligible?)\n    47\t             ((get descriptor :yang.call/eligible?) callable)\n    48\t             true))))]\n    49\t    [yang.call/invocation\n    50\t     (fn [callee positional named raise]\n    51\t       (assoc (assoc (assoc (assoc {} :yang.call/callee callee)\n    52\t                            :yang.call/positional positional)\n    53\t                     :yang.call/named named)\n    54\t              :yang.call/raise raise))]\n    55\t    [yang.call/invocation-with\n    56\t     (fn [callee positional named raise options]\n    57\t       (assoc (assoc (assoc (assoc options :yang.call/callee callee)\n    58\t                            :yang.call/positional positional)\n    59\t                     :yang.call/named named)\n    60\t              :yang.call/raise raise))]\n    61\t    [yang.call/_pair?\n    62\t     (fn [pair]\n    63\t       (if (data/vector? pair)\n    64\t         (= (data/count pair) 2)\n    65\t         false))]\n  2600\t   'coll? [1], 'set? [1]})\n  2601\t\n  2602\t\n  2603\t(def library\n  2604\t  \"The library's definitions, in order, as quoted source.\"\n  2605\t  '[(defn identity\n  2606\t      [x]\n  2607\t      x)\n  2608\t    (defn inc\n  2609\t      [x]\n  2610\t      (+ x 1))\n  2611\t    (defn dec\n  2612\t      [x]\n  2613\t      (- x 1))\n  2614\t    (defn second\n  2615\t      [x]\n  2616\t      (first (rest x)))\n  2617\t    (defn not=\n  2618\t      [a b]\n  2619\t      (not (= a b)))\n  2620\t    (defn boolean?\n  2621\t      [x]\n  2622\t      (or (= x true) (= x false)))\n  2623\t    (defn fn?\n  2624\t      [x]\n  2625\t      (if (data/callable? x) true (yang.call/eligible? x)))\n  2626\t    (defn ^:yin.k/invoker invoke-value\n  2627\t      [f args]\n  2628\t      (cond\n  2629\t        (vector? f) (if (= 1 (count args)) (nth f (first args))\n  2630\t                        (throw (ex-info \"Vector invocation requires one index\" {:argc (count args)})))\n   169\t**Capture-safety for bundled `yang.clojure`:**\n   170\t- In `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2470–2517`, the live preamble currently constructs **four** portable guest helpers (`dsg__get`, `dsg__equal`, `dsg__assoc`, `dsg__empty`), while `conj` and `vec` are protected against local shadowing by the convention's binder checks (`clojure.cljc:2479`), and `+` / `<=` currently have no `dsg__*` helpers.\n   171\t- When `yrt.escape/uast` is bundled inline into non-linked `yang.clojure`, it reads `get`, `=`, `assoc`, `conj`, `+`, and `<=`. To preserve capture-safety without breaking UCF machine-data validation (never binding host primitive functions inside guest environments):\n   172\t  - `[get = assoc]` bind to the existing portable guest closures `[dsg__get dsg__equal dsg__assoc]`;\n   173\t  - `conj` continues to rely on `yang.clojure`'s binder-check refusal;\n   174\t  - `+` and `<=` are wrapped by adding two portable guest closures `dsg__add` and `dsg__lte` (which use raw `(+ a b)` / `(<= a b)` when unshadowed, or safe binary integer fallback / binder-check refusal when `+` or `<=` is defined in `(::defined env)` — all pure guest lambdas, matching `dsg__get` at `clojure.cljc:2484`).\n   175\t- In linked mode, module lexical isolation makes the `dsg__*` wrapper unnecessary.\n   176\t\n   177\t### 4.3 `yrt.float` — IEEE-754 float64 math seam\n   178\t\n   179\tUsed by: `py`, `js`, `php`, `clj.py` (omitted by `clj`). Host dependencies: `data/float64`, `data/float-value`, `data/content=`, `data/count`, `data/nth`, `data/str-concat`, `integer/shift-right`, `integer/bit-and`, `integer/sub`, `integer/floor-div-mod`, `integer/float-digits`, `integer/decimal->float`.\n   180\t\n   181\t**Representational, domain, and no-capture rules:**\n   182\t1. **Unwrapped double helpers vs. `data/float64` exports:**\n   183\t   - Every exported constant (`inf`, `neg-inf`, `nan`, `zero`, `neg-zero`) and boxed arithmetic result returns a **Jing `data/float64` carrier** (or bare host double specifically for `*-raw` leaf helpers whose contract states raw binary64 input/output). `yrt.float` never inspects or emits `{:py/float ...}` tags; `py` wraps/unwraps at its thin boundary (owner decision D3).\n   184\t   - **No-capture / no-safepoint rule:** All `*-raw` helpers acting on unwrapped host doubles (`data/float-value`) are pure leaf computations that **must not invoke `%capture`, safepoints, or arbitrary guest closures while an unwrapped double is live on the stack/in an environment**. Policy callbacks (`on-underflow`, `on-range`) take safe values (never returning unwrapped doubles across a continuation boundary) and are invoked only at explicit guard boundaries.\n   185\t   - Preserves `yang.antlr.md` §8.5.5: zero bare integral float literals (`(data/float-value 1)` instead of `1.0`), `-0.0` constructed as `(* (data/float-value -1) (data/float-value 0))`.\n   186\t2. **Exact exponentiation rounding reconciliation (`fpow-half` vs. `fpow-base`):**\n   187\t   - Live `py/fpow` (`yang.python.antlr.prelude:1514`) and `js/fpow` (`yang.typescript.antlr.prelude:617`) recursively compute the half-power `h = fpow(base, floor(e/2))` and square `h * h` (multiplying by `base` when `e` is odd).\n   188\t   - Live `php/pow-sq` (`yang.php.antlr.prelude:1361`) squares the base first (`pow-sq(x * x, floor(e/2))`) and multiplies by `x` when `e` is odd.\n   189\t   - Those two evaluation orders round differently at the final ULP — e.g. for `1.1 ** 6`, half-power squaring yields `0x1.c585058dde7acp+0` while base squaring yields `0x1.c585058dde7abp+0`.\n   190\t   - To avoid silently breaking PHP or Python/JS oracle parity in P1, `yrt.float` exports **both** `fpow-half` (for `py`/`js`/`clj.py`) and `fpow-base` (for `php`) — see owner decision D7 if a future unified rounding rule is ever approved.\n   191\t\n   192\t**Public exports and domain preconditions:**\n   193\t\n   194\t| Export | Preconditions / Domain | Behavior & Policy Hooks |\n   195\t|---|---|---|\n   196\t| `inf`, `neg-inf`, `nan`, `zero`, `neg-zero` | — | Return `data/float64` boxed constants |\n   197\t| `nan?`, `finite?`, `zero?`, `neg-zero?`, `abs`, `neg`, `zero-like` | Unwrapped host double `x` (plus sign target `y` for `zero-like`) | `nan?`: `(not (<= x x))`; `finite?`: `(< (abs x) (data/float-value ##Inf))`; `zero-like`: `copysign(0.0, y)` |\n   198\t| `fdiv` | Unwrapped doubles `x, y` (NaN/Inf permitted) | IEEE division preserving `y`'s signed zero on overflow to `±Inf` and `0/0 -> NaN` |\n   199\t| `fmod-pos` | **Requires finite `x >= 0` and finite `y > 0`** (callers exclude NaN, `±Inf`, `y = 0` beforehand) | Exact Sterbenz long-division remainder loop |\n   200\t| `fpow-half` / `fpow-base` | Unwrapped double `base`, exact integer `e >= 0` | Integer-exponent squaring (`py`/`js` order vs. `php` order) |\n   201\t| `neg-pow` | Unwrapped double `x` (finite nonzero caller-checked or IEEE), exact integer `n > 0`, `[x n] -> result` hook `on-underflow`, squaring fn | Computes `1 / x^n`; splits into halves `h` and `n - h` when `x^n` overflows to `Inf`; calls `(on-underflow x n)` when `x^n` underflows to `0.0` (so `py`/`js` raise `OverflowError` or compute `±Inf` while `php` returns signed `±Inf`) |\n   202\t| `floor-int`, `float-floor` | Unwrapped double `x`, `[x] -> result` hook `on-range` | Exact binary descent for finite `|x| < 2^53`; calls `(on-range x)` if out of supported range |\n   203\t| `float-parts` | **Requires finite nonzero double `x`** (caller excludes `0.0`, `NaN`, `±Inf`) | Returns exact `[mantissa exp2]` integer decomposition |\n   204\t| `zeros`, `exp-text` | Exact counts/exponents | Decimal string layout helpers over `integer/float-digits` |\n   205\t\n   206\t### 4.4 `yrt.int` — exact-integer helpers and fixed-width wrapping\n   207\t\n   208\tUsed by: `py`, `js`, `php`, `clj.py` (omitted by `clj`). Host dependencies: `data/count`, `data/nth`, `integer/*`.\n   209\t\n   210\t**`yin.vm.integer` v4 refusal algebra (`src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:81–100`):**\n   211\t- `yin.vm.integer` v4 **returns** keyword reasons in place of a result for exactly **three** resource/range conditions: `:yin.vm.integer/bit-limit`, `:yin.vm.integer/digit-limit`, and `:yin.vm.integer/float-overflow`.\n   212\t- Every other refusal (`:arity`, `:wrong-type`, `:out-of-range`, `:syntax`, `:zero-division`, `:negative-count`, `:negative-exponent`) **throws** a host operational failure indicating a caller-side defect (`integer.cljc:93–100`) and **MUST NOT** be caught or relabeled into guest exceptions.\n   213\t\n   214\t**Public exports:**\n   215\t- **`check-limit`** (`[res on-bit-limit on-digit-limit on-float-overflow]`): inspects `res` strictly against the three returned keywords (`:yin.vm.integer/bit-limit`, `:yin.vm.integer/digit-limit`, `:yin.vm.integer/float-overflow`), invoking the matching policy closure and returning `res` unchanged for every valid integer, float, boolean, string, or `[q r]` pair.\n   216\t  - *(Language-specific adapter ownership:)* `php/int-result` (`yang.php.antlr.prelude:902`) performs signed-64-bit demotion to float (`(if (php/in-range? n) n (php/f (integer/to-float n)))`) rather than Python/JS's exception mapping; PHP therefore keeps `php/int-result` in Layer 2b built over `yrt.int/in-range?` and `yrt.int/check-limit`.\n   217\t- **Fixed-width wrapping:** `wrap-unsigned` (`[x width-pow2 mask]`) and `wrap-signed` (`[x half-range range]`), backing `js/wrap32` / `js/to-int32` / `js/to-uint32` and `php/wrap64`; `in-range?` (`[x min-v max-v]`).\n   218\t- **Generic digit & scanner primitives:** `digit-value` (`[code-point]`), `all-digits?` (`[cs i n radix]`), `scan-digits` (`[cs i n radix acc]`), `skip-while` (`[cs i n pred?]`), `skip-zeros` (`[cs i n]`). Each language supplies its own whitespace predicate (`pred?`), sign, prefix, and underscore syntax rules in Layer 2b.\n   219\t- **Admission ownership:** Per-language integer floors (`min-integer-bits = 53` for `py`/`js`/`clj.py`; `min-integer-bits = 4096, min-integer-digits = 1000` for `php`) stay enforced by each language profile's admission check, not hardcoded inside `yrt.int`.\n   637\t\n   638\t1. **Address-neutral slices (P0, P2, P3):** `P0` preserves exact pre-change UAST bytes on frozen snapshots; `P2` emits byte-identical UAST for every default language profile; `P3` adds `:yang.python/clojure-core-v1` and `clojure.core` without changing `yang.clojure`'s default `library-for` output or any existing language goldens.\n   639\t2. **One batched profile bump (`P1`):** Each `yrt.*` module (`yrt.escape`, `yrt.float`, `yrt.int`) is implemented and validated independently first, then activated across `py`, `js`, `php`, and `clj` in one coordinated profile bump so `:segment/blake3-*` goldens (`scanner_goldens.cljc`, `float_address_test.cljc`, `dht-import-v1.edn`, `clojure_callsite_test.cljc`, linked harnesses) refresh once.\n   640\t\n   641\t---\n   642\t\n   643\t## 9. Phased implementation roadmap and acceptance gates\n   644\t\n   645\t| Slice | Scope & deliverables | Golden impact | Concrete acceptance gate |\n   646\t|---|---|---|---|\n   647\t| **P0 — Composition plumbing & `defquoted` consolidation** | Consolidate `defquoted` into `yang.core.prelude`; implement `:yang.prelude/recipe` / `:yang.prelude/profile` composition and verified-closure validation in `yang.core.prelude` + `prelude-admission` (preserving operand sequencing, `init!` placement, tail marking, `:linear`/`:balanced` mode, and Clojure's custom preamble). | **Zero** (byte-identical emission) | Compare generated UAST/specs against frozen pre-change snapshots of `py`/`js`/`php`/`clj`/`yang.call`; test verified-closure edge validation, missing transitive artifact/export refusal, provenance-door/effect-ceiling exclusions, and projected-name collision refusal. |\n   648\t| **P1 — Layer 2a Tier 1 (`yrt.escape`, `yrt.float`, `yrt.int`)** | Implement the three stateless guest modules; migrate `js`/`clj` from 4-slot to 5-slot contexts (`:handlers :depth :base :frame`); preserve open non-`:finally` (`:generator`) boundary dispatch; add `dsg__add`/`dsg__lte` guest helpers for bundled `clj`; activate across `py`, `js`, `php`, `clj` in one coordinated bump. | **One batched bump** | Unit tests for `yrt.*` on semantic/stack/register VMs; existing Python & PHP generator tests green; 4→5-slot migration tests; PHP `abandon-to` / `call-terminal` & normal `finally` replacement (`:py/None`, `:js/undefined`, `:php/null`, `nil`); `1.1 ** 6` bit-pattern parity (`fpow-half` vs `fpow-base`); `yin.vm.integer` v4 three-reason translation without masking operational throws; no-capture float seams; independent concurrent `py`/`js`/`php`/`clj` tasks sharing `yrt.*`; bundled == linked + 4-host + CPython/Node/PHP oracle parity; `clj ./bin/analyze-deps.clj` clean. |\n   649\t| **P2 — Lowering parameterization (`yang.core.semantics` + frontend contracts)** | Implement generic `yang.core.semantics`; define `yang.python.antlr.contract` (then TS/PHP); refactor `yang.python.antlr.lower` to read literals/hooks/capabilities/projections from `:yang.semantics/descriptor` defaulting to `:yang.python/py-v1`. | **Zero** (byte-identical default output) | Byte-for-byte UAST equality test across the full corpus under `:yang.python/py-v1`; tests covering every supported lowering path and capability mode (`:cell`, `:single-assignment`, `:ambient-only`), unqualified-hook rejection, reserved-door projection ban, and explicit refusal of generator expressions when `:control/generators :refused`. |\n   650\t| **P3 — `clojure.core` prelude for `yang.python` (`:yang.python/clojure-core-v1`)** | Publish standalone portable `clojure.core` module (`§7.5`) + `clj.py` adapter + `:yang.python/clojure-core-v1` descriptor; leave `yang.clojure`'s default `library-for` untouched; wire `yang.frontend` multi-revision catalog selection. | **Zero** on existing languages | Cross-unit callable admission & forgery tests (plain maps, forged cell-refs rejected; `clj.py/make-fn` admitted by `yang.clojure` / `clojure.core` / `py` / `js` / `php`); lexical shadowing of projected names (`conj`, `is_empty`, `map`) leaving lowered UAST uncaptured; primitive callable projections (`filter(is_empty, ...)`); per-module `globals` isolation across two units; `[:yin.k/ok 42]` ordinary data returned unstripped; condition-shaped user throws vs. retained protocol exceptions (`A→B→A` `finally` cleanup); portable `clojure.core` subset excluding `var?`/`resolve`; 4-host execution parity. |\n   651\t| **P4 — Tier-2 shared modules (`yrt.gen`, `yrt.safepoint`)** | Extract parameterized `yrt.gen` (preserving Python `:base` rebasing vs. PHP direct context restoration and refcount cleanup) and `yrt.safepoint` (`stream/poll!` skeleton for `pysp`/`jssp`). | **One bump** (deferred) | Generator + safepoint parity suites across VMs/hosts, preserving PHP lifecycle/cleanup policy and non-rebasing context behavior vs. Python rebasing. |\n   652\t\n   653\t---\n   654\t\n   655\t## 10. Open owner decisions\n   656\t\n   657\t1. **D1 — Local mutation model for `:yang.python/clojure-core-v1`:**\n   658\t   - *Option A (Recommended — Architect concurred):* `:mutation/locals :cell` (task-heap cells holding pure persistent Clojure values, matching Python parameter binding, unbound reads, reassignment, loops, and `nonlocal` with per-module `globals` cells). Keep `:single-assignment` as a separate restricted profile.\n   659\t   - *Option B:* `:mutation/locals :single-assignment` only (refuses local reassignment).\n   660\t2. **D2 — Namespace prefix for Layer-2a shared modules:**\n   661\t   - *Option A (Recommended — Architect concurred):* `yrt.*` (`yrt.escape`, `yrt.float`, `yrt.int`, `yrt.gen`, `yrt.safepoint`) matching `yang.typescript.md` §17 and `yang.php.md` §9, implemented in Clojure under `src/cljc/yang/rt/*.cljc`.\n   662\t   - *Option B:* `yang.rt.*` for both Clojure namespaces and linker module names.\n   663\t3. **D3 — Python float tagging during P1:**\n   664\t   - *Option A (Recommended — Architect concurred):* Retain `{:py/float x}` behind a 2-line `py` boundary adapter around `yrt.float` so P1 stays a pure runtime extraction without shifting Python value semantics or equality.\n   665\t   - *Option B:* Migrate Python to untagged `data/float64` carriers in P1.\n   666\t4. **D4 — Batching P1 (`yrt.escape` + `yrt.float` + `yrt.int`) vs. per-module bumps:**\n   667\t   - *Option A (Recommended conditionally — Architect concurred):* Implement and test each `yrt.*` extraction independently first, then activate all three in one coordinated profile bump so `:segment/blake3-*` goldens refresh once.\n   668\t   - *Option B:* Ship each `yrt.*` module as a separate profile bump.\n   669\t5. **D5 — Cross-syntax identifier mapping for Clojure names (`?`, `!`, `-`):**\n   670\t   - *Option A (Recommended — Architect concurred):* Curated `:yang.semantics/ambient-projections` table validated against exact exports, lexical precedence, primitive callable projections, and reserved-door bans.\n   671\t   - *Option B:* Algorithmic mangling rule (`_q` / `_bang`).\n   672\t6. **D6 — Publishing `clojure.core` as a standalone linked module:**\n   673\t   - *Option A (Constrained, Recommended — Architect concurred):* Publish the portable `clojure.core` subset (`§7.5`) with per-task instantiation in P3 for `:yang.python/clojure-core-v1` and future profiles, while keeping `yang.clojure`'s default `library-for` bundling unchanged in P3 (migrating `yang.clojure` separately later so P3 moves zero Clojure goldens).\n   674\t   - *Option B:* Copy needed `library` definitions into `clj.py`.\n   675\t7. **D7 — Float exponentiation rounding (`py`/`js` half-power vs. `php` base squaring):**\n   676\t   - *Option A (Recommended for P1):* Export both `yrt.float/fpow-half` and `yrt.float/fpow-base` so P1 preserves bit-exact parity (`1.1 ** 6`: `0x1.c585058dde7acp+0` for `py`/`js` vs `0x1.c585058dde7abp+0` for `php`) across all existing oracles.\n   677\t   - *Option B:* Explicitly approve unifying PHP onto `fpow-half` (or `py`/`js` onto `fpow-base`) and update the affected language's oracle departure ledger.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
