=====================================================\n   415\t;; Kinds\n   416\t;; =============================================================================\n   417\t\n   418\t(defn- data-number?\n   419\t  \"True when `x` is a host number or an exact-integer carrier, including\n   420\t   a JS or Dart big integer, which answers false to `number?`. A\n   421\t   closure, a continuation, nil and a map are not, whatever they answer\n   422\t   to `get`.\"\n   423\t  [x]\n   424\t  (or (number? x) (integer-host/big-carrier? x)))\n   425\t\n   426\t\n   427\t;; =============================================================================\n   428\t;; Floats: the seam between addressed float content and host arithmetic\n   429\t;; =============================================================================\n   430\t\n   431\t(defn float-value\n   432\t  \"The host double of number or float64 content `x`, for host arithmetic:\n   433\t   what the literal `1.0` is on each host, a double on the JVM and Dart, a\n   434\t   Number on JS. A prelude unwraps a float with this, computes on bare\n   435\t   numbers, and wraps the result back with `float64`.\"\n   436\t  [x]\n   437\t  (when-not (or (data-number? x) (cbor/float64? x))\n   438\t    (wrong-type! 'float-value 0 :number))\n   439\t  #?(:cljd (if (dart/is? x BigInt) (.toDouble ^BigInt x) (.toDouble ^num x))\n   440\t     :clj (double x)\n   441\t     :cljs (cond (number? x) x\n   442\t                 (cbor/float64? x) (.-v ^not-native x)\n   443\t                 :else (js/Number x))))\n   444\t\n   445\t\n   446\t(defn float64\n   447\t  \"Float content with the value of number `x`: Jing's float64, the form a\n   448\t   float keeps in any value that can reach an addressed row or image (a\n   449\t   double on the JVM and Dart, the float64 carrier on JS, where a bare\n   450\t   integral Number is an integer; dao.jing.cbor.md, Numeric identity). The\n   451\t   producer carries float kind; the codec never infers it.\"\n   452\t  [x]\n   453\t  (cbor/float64 (float-value x)))\n   454\t\n   455\t\n   456\t(defn- callable?\n21:                      the same value as float64 (`js/kind-of`)\n88:           data/number? data/nth data/str-concat data/str-length data/substring\n118:    [js/kind-of\n132:               (if (data/number? x)\n306:       (let [k (js/kind-of v)]\n314:       (let [k (js/kind-of v)]\n337:       (let [k (js/kind-of v)]\n504:       (let [k (js/kind-of v)]\n520:     (fn [p] (if (= (js/kind-of p) :bigint) p (js/to-number p)))]\n563:       (if (= (js/kind-of x) :number)\n564:         (if (= (js/kind-of y) :number) (numop x y) (js/mix-error))\n565:         (if (= (js/kind-of y) :number) (js/mix-error) (bigop x y))))]\n715:         (if (= (js/kind-of x) :number)\n740:         (if (= (js/kind-of x) :number)\n781:       (let [k (js/kind-of a)]\n782:         (if (= k (js/kind-of b))\n789:       (let [k (js/kind-of a)]\n790:         (if (= k (js/kind-of b))\n797:       (let [k (js/kind-of a)]\n798:         (if (= k (js/kind-of b))\n815:       (let [ta (js/ty (js/kind-of a))\n816:             tb (js/ty (js/kind-of b))]\n866:       (let [kx (js/kind-of nx)\n867:             ky (js/kind-of ny)]\n881:         (if (if (= (js/kind-of px) :bigint) (js/str? py) false)\nsrc/cljc/dao/host/number.cljc\nr3-fix-wt/src/cljc/dao/host/number.cljc\n3707:no binding authority.\n3716:validates extension fields under its admitted binding profile; the shared\n3752:The callee entry installs its exception boundary **before parameter\n3753:binding**. It owns defaults, arity checks, positional/named/variadic\n3754:binding, receiver interpretation, and supported source constraints and\n3765:| Python function | Python binding/default/keyword rules and captured module globals. A missing required argument raises the Python binding error. |\n3767:| PHP function | Named-parameter, static-state, reference and declared type rules. Function self comes from `:yang.call/callee`, not the JavaScript receiver. |\n3768:| Further frontend | Its declared binding rules, with no new case in the shared prelude or evaluator. |\n3802:  writable variable binding.\n3819:authorize rebinding the caller's variable: a value read from a local is\n3822:Source-local reference arguments keep their pinned native binding\n3824:compatible profile or fail with a guest binding error before the body.\n3863:`:yang.call/unsupported-binding`, with plain evidence. The caller maps\n3865:original condition. Callee argument-binding failures retain the actual\n3900:`:yang.call/thrown`; its local exception for a protocol failure must still\n3929:must not repeat parameter binding, argument evaluation, defaults,\n3960:Lowering/runtime profiles pin the implementation and binding/value\n3963:Do not duplicate existing lambda parameters in another persisted\n3976:only after proving equivalent evaluation, binding, foreign callability,\n3997:   preserve source metadata/private code while putting binding and\n4000:   private body; generated entry/binding boilerplate remains unmarked.\n4043:- **Presence and binding:** distinguish missing from explicit\n4044:  null/None/undefined; retain duplicate keywords until binding; supply\n4060:- **Exceptions:** cover binding failures, arbitrary foreign thrown data,\n4079:Report callable kinds, binding/value extensions, actual host/VM\n4225:Value-semantics languages map as follows. PHP reference binding cannot be\n4226:a lexical rebinding of the name, since a continuation captured earlier\n4227:would still see the old binding; that is why the reference marker lives\n4366:encodings, truthiness, equality, arithmetic semantics, argument binding,\n4624:- Scope analysis, argument binding, truthiness, operators, sequencing,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc | sed -n '110,145p'\nrg -n 'Float64|float64-of|IEquiv|valueOf' src/cljc/dao/host/number.cljc\nnl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '3855,3874p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yang.preludes.md | sed -n '260,281p'\nrg -n 'data/number\\\\?|float64\\\\?' src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc | tail -15\nnl -ba collab/yang-base/1795169993265-architect-yang-preludes-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | sed -n '15,115p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc | sed -n '110,145p'\nrg -n 'Float64|float64-of|IEquiv|valueOf' src/cljc/dao/host/number.cljc\nnl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '3855,3874p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yang.preludes.md | sed -n '260,281p'\nrg -n 'data/number\\\\?|float64\\\\?' src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc | tail -15\nnl -ba collab/yang-base/1795169993265-architect-yang-preludes-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | sed -n '15,115p'\"","aggregated_output":"   110\t     (fn [description]\n   111\t       (cell/new (assoc (assoc {} :js/type :symbol) :description description)))]\n   112\t    [js/str?\n   113\t     (fn [x]\n   114\t       (if (nil? (get x :js/str)) (not (nil? (get x :js/units))) true))]\n   115\t    [js/pair (fn [a b] (conj (conj [] a) b))]\n   116\t    [js/vconj (fn [xs x] (conj xs x))]\n   117\t    [js/arg (fn [args i] (get args i :js/undefined))]\n   118\t    [js/kind-of\n   119\t     ;; undefined null boolean number bigint string function object. A\n   120\t     ;; number is float64 content, a bigint a bare exact integer: content\n   121\t     ;; equality with the same value as float64 tells them apart on every\n   122\t     ;; host (the JS carrier is not a host number at all)\n   123\t     (fn [x]\n   124\t       (if (= x :js/undefined)\n   125\t         :undefined\n   126\t         (if (= x :js/null)\n   127\t           :null\n   128\t           (if (= x true)\n   129\t             :boolean\n   130\t             (if (= x false)\n   131\t               :boolean\n   132\t               (if (data/number? x)\n   133\t                 (if (data/content= x (data/float64 x)) :number :bigint)\n   134\t                 (if (js/str? x)\n   135\t                   :string\n   136\t                   (if (if (js/cell? x) (cell/authentic? x) false)\n   137\t                     (if (= (get (cell/get x) :js/type) :function)\n   138\t                       :function\n   139\t                       (if (= (get (cell/get x) :js/type) :symbol)\n   140\t                         :symbol\n   141\t                         ;; A cell-backed foreign callable (a python\n   142\t                         ;; or PHP function: a cell whose contents\n   143\t                         ;; carry a call entry, no :js/type) is a\n   144\t                         ;; function too, not an object.\n   145\t                         (if (yang.call/eligible? x) :function :object)))\n14:   | float64  | `Double`                    | `Float64` carrier  | `double`   |\n19:   Table C laws of the carriers (a port must meet them): `Float64` is\n20:   equal to another `Float64` iff their bits are equal, every NaN equal to\n23:   (`valueOf`) `:carrier-coercion`. `Rational` is equal iff numerator and\n69:       (.setFloat64 view 0 d)\n76:   (deftype Float64\n89:     (valueOf\n91:       (refuse 'Float64 :carrier-coercion\n95:     IEquiv\n99:       (and (instance? Float64 other)\n100:            (or (and (js/isNaN v) (js/isNaN (.-v ^Float64 other)))\n101:                (js/Object.is v (.-v ^Float64 other)))))\n118:  #?@(:cljd [cljd.core/IEquiv\n134:      :cljs [IEquiv\n151:     cljd.core/IEquiv\n167:     IEquiv\n200:(defn float64-of\n202:   `Double` on the JVM, a `double` on Dart, the `Float64` carrier on JS\n203:   (so integral floats such as 1.0 keep their kind); a JS `Float64` is\n208:     :cljs (Float64. (if (instance? Float64 x) (.-v x) (js/Number x)))))\n212:  \"P. The host double of floating-point content `x` (on JS, a `Float64`\n217:     :cljs (if (instance? Float64 x) (.-v x) x)))\n  3855\tordinary return. Returning an identical-looking map returns normal data.\n  3856\tLikewise, a source program throwing a map with these keys throws that\n  3857\tmap as the **payload of a new condition**; its shape is never evidence\n  3858\tthat it is already a condition to forward. No engine-effect value is\n  3859\tsynthesized.\n  3860\t\n  3861\tProtocol failures use qualified reasons such as\n  3862\t`:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`, and\n  3863\t`:yang.call/unsupported-binding`, with plain evidence. The caller maps\n  3864\tthem to a documented local exception form retaining the complete\n  3865\toriginal condition. Callee argument-binding failures retain the actual\n  3866\tcallee exception under `:yang.call/thrown`. These reasons do not extend\n  3867\t`dao.stream`'s outcome algebra.\n  3868\t\n  3869\tThe receiving caller interprets an escaping exception as follows:\n  3870\t\n  3871\t1. A payload valid as an exception in its own runtime retains that\n  3872\t   runtime's ordinary catching and identity behavior.\n  3873\t2. A runtime permitting arbitrary thrown values, such as JavaScript,\n  3874\t   may deliver the original payload directly.\n   260\t    :yang.prelude/refusal-ns \"yang.python.antlr\"}\n   261\t   ```\n   262\t2. **`:yang.prelude/profile` (immutable, content-addressed runtime profile after publication):**\n   263\t   Replaces the recipe's local build knobs with the **published content address of the owning runtime module itself** (`:yang.prelude/root-address`) alongside the pinned transitive closure:\n   264\t   ```clojure\n   265\t   {:yang.prelude/schema 1\n   266\t    :yang.prelude/id :yang.python/py-prelude\n   267\t    :yang.prelude/revision \"2026-11-20.1\"\n   268\t    :yang.prelude/module 'py\n   269\t    :yang.prelude/root-address \"<blake3-address-of-published-py-module>\"\n   270\t    :yang.prelude/components\n   271\t    [[yang.call \"<blake3-address>\"]\n   272\t     [yrt.escape \"<blake3-address>\"]\n   273\t     [yrt.float \"<blake3-address>\"]\n   274\t     [yrt.int \"<blake3-address>\"]]\n   275\t    :yang.prelude/host-modules\n   276\t    {:cell {:required true}\n   277\t     :data {:required true, :min-max-items 1048576}\n   278\t     :integer {:required true, :min-bits 53}}\n   279\t    :yang.prelude/max-effects #{}} ;; optional allowed-effect ceiling (nil if none beyond derived footprint)\n   280\t   ```\n   281\t   A `:yang.prelude/profile` contains zero host functions or closures and passes `yang.frontend/non-portable-path`. Its `:yang.prelude/root-address` ensures two profiles cannot claim identical identity while pointing at different owning prelude definitions.\n437:  (when-not (or (data-number? x) (cbor/float64? x))\n442:                 (cbor/float64? x) (.-v ^not-native x)\n    15\tThe shared-runtime decomposition is architecturally viable. The proposed Python-to-Clojure profile, however, contains incompatible numeric, calling, binding, and publication contracts. Several P1 extraction assumptions also contradict live code.\n    16\t\n    17\tThese are design defects requiring reconciliation before implementation, not objections that proposed namespaces do not yet exist. P4’s deferral is reasonable, but P1 must preserve existing generators.\n    18\t\n    19\tReview was read-only. Evidence comprises source inspection and a small, in-memory numerical reproduction; repository test suites were not run.\n    20\t\n    21\t**2. Findings**\n    22\t\n    23\t**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:287](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:287) | The Clojure profile’s numeric hooks violate four-host portability | Define a portable numeric contract and adapters.**\n    24\t\n    25\tThe descriptor emits float64 carriers but maps arithmetic and comparisons directly to bare `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, and comparison primitives. `yang.antlr.md` §8.5.5 explicitly states that generic arithmetic on decoded carriers is not portable: Node refuses carrier coercion while JVM/Dart can compute. Thus even `2.0 + 1.0` contradicts P3’s portability claim.\n    26\t\n    27\tThe “exact integer” claim also needs more than `clj.py/int-lit`: subsequent operations must preserve exactness and obey integer limits. [yin.vm.integer:9](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:9) requires guest integer arithmetic through its kernels.\n    28\t\n    29\tSpecify operand kinds, mixed numeric operations, equality, division results, overflow/refusal behavior, and float reboxing. Alternatively, explicitly refuse unsupported numeric operations in this profile.\n    30\t\n    31\t**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:310](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:310) | Hook substitution does not define a compatible lowering ABI | Specify frontend-owned hook signatures and structural lowering rules.**\n    32\t\n    33\tThe proposed mappings are not interchangeable with current Python calls:\n    34\t\n    35\t- Python global reads/writes pass a module dictionary and name; `clj/global-get` and `clj/define-global!` take only a name, or name/value.\n    36\t- Python `try` takes body, handler, and `orelse`; `clj/try` takes two arguments. Direct replacement loses `try/except/else` behavior.\n    37\t- Python’s unit body receives `%globals` and `%globals-fn`; `clj/run-main` invokes a zero-argument body and removes one `[:yin.k/ok value]` wrapper.\n    38\t- Python function construction includes parameter specifications, definition-time defaults, keyword defaults, and an argument-vector body.\n    39\t\n    40\tSee [Python lowering:801](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:801), [function-value:1836](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:1836), and [Clojure runtime:136](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:136).\n    41\t\n    42\tThe example also uses `%`, but the descriptor supplies no remainder hook. Comprehensions, exception matching, unpacking, and parameter-binding capabilities need explicit support or refusal.\n    43\t\n    44\tKeep these contracts in the installed frontend implementation or a frontend-supplied contract description. Making Yang core understand Python constructs would undermine the open SPI.\n    45\t\n    46\t**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:390](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:390) | Shared entry shape does not establish Clojure callable admission or exception bridging | Specify trusted constructor and call-boundary behavior.**\n    47\t\n    48\tExisting Clojure functions are not merely maps with `:yang.call/entry`. The compiler constructs admission witnesses and private invocation tokens; dynamic calls validate admission. See [descriptor construction:1282](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1282) and [admission checking:2409](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2409). A new immutable `clj.py` descriptor does not automatically satisfy separately compiled `clojure.core` consumers.\n    49\t\n    50\tLikewise, passing `clj/raise` directly as the common raise exit is insufficient. It accepts a local thrown value, whereas `yang.call` transfers a condition. Existing [compile-guest-call:1427](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1427) unwraps ordinary thrown payloads and privately retains protocol-condition provenance.\n    51\t\n    52\tDefine admission across independently compiled units, public invocation-record handling, binding-before-body exception protection, and condition conversion. Keep provenance operations inside trusted generated Layer-2b boundaries. Require tests for forged descriptors, condition-shaped thrown data, retained protocol exceptions, and A→B→A cleanup.\n    53\t\n    54\t**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:427](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:427) | Publishing the entire existing library is neither a complete export plan nor a portable module plan | Publish an explicit portable surface with task-instantiation rules.**\n    55\t\n    56\t`yang.clojure/library` is quoted source currently selected transitively by `library-for`. Publishing all of it changes admission requirements: `resolve` references unavailable `kind/resolve`, and `var?` requires `kind/var?`, which the supplied registry installs only on CLJ. See [kind-module:2578](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2578) and [library:2677](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2677).\n    57\t\n    58\tSeveral promised names also are not definitions in that library: `empty?` is a VM primitive, while `assoc-in`, `update-in`, `set`, and `hash-map` are not supplied by the cited library/primitive surfaces.\n    59\t\n    60\tSpecify the export inventory, primitive-to-callable projections, portable subset, qualified sibling references, initialization, and per-task callable construction. Preserve library-internal bindings against caller global redefinition. Linking a module must not implicitly project all its exports into a source namespace.\n    61\t\n    62\t**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:135](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:135) | The escape-frame contract excludes existing generator boundaries | Preserve generator frames during P1.**\n    63\t\n    64\tThe proposed frame kind is restricted to `:handler` or `:finally`. Both [Python gen-start:466](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:466) and [PHP gen-start:5309](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5309) install `:generator` frames today. Existing raise logic dispatches every non-finally frame to its payload.\n    65\t\n    66\tEither retain that handler-equivalent behavior or explicitly migrate generator boundary construction to the new representation. Deferring generator extraction does not defer compatibility.\n    67\t\n    68\tAlso make the four-to-five-slot migration explicit: current JS and Clojure contexts contain only `:handlers`. Distinguish frame count `n` from activation depth. The P4 description should not claim existing PHP generator rebasing matches Python: PHP currently restores its saved context directly; Python recalculates the crossing base.\n    69\t\n    70\t**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:305](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:305) | Descriptor hooks introduce lexical capture and insufficient authority validation | Make generated references hygienic and validate exact authorized exports.**\n    71\t\n    72\tCurrent Python lowering deliberately emits protected qualified runtime names. Replacing collection construction with bare `conj` permits a valid Python parameter named `conj` to capture generated machinery. Alias resolution also needs a defined precedence relative to locals, `nonlocal`, globals, and ambient defaults.\n    73\t\n    74\tNamespace coverage in §6.3 proves neither export existence nor permission to expose it. The `yrt.*` provenance prohibition does not itself stop a descriptor alias or source-visible projection from exposing reserved `cell/dsg__*` doors. Registry availability is not source-level authority.\n    75\t\n    76\tRequire hygienic generated references, scope-aware alias resolution, exact export/profile checks, and reserved-door restrictions for source-facing projections.\n    77\t\n    78\tThe bundled Clojure wrapper needs a precise helper plan too. The existing preamble supplies four named helpers, and source `conj`/`vec` shadowing is already refused; the document’s claim that all listed primitives have existing `dsg__*` replacements is inaccurate. New helpers must remain portable guest closures, not captured host functions.\n    79\t\n    80\t**MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:169](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:169) | Float extraction assumes equivalence that live implementations do not have | Preserve evaluation order or explicitly approve a semantic change.**\n    81\t\n    82\tPython/JS exponentiation recursively computes a half-power and squares its result. PHP squares the base before recursion. These orders round differently.\n    83\t\n    84\tAn in-memory reproduction of the source algorithms for `1.1 ** 6` produced:\n    85\t\n    86\t| Algorithm | Binary64 result |\n    87\t|---|---|\n    88\t| Python/JS | `0x1.c585058dde7acp+0` |\n    89\t| PHP | `0x1.c585058dde7abp+0` |\n    90\t\n    91\tSee [Python fpow:1514](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1514) and [PHP pow-sq:1361](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:1361). One shared implementation cannot preserve both results unchanged.\n    92\t\n    93\tSpecify signed underflow/overflow behavior, NaN and infinity preconditions, and the finite-input domain of `fmod-pos`. Reinstate the explicit no-capture/no-safepoint rule around unwrapped arithmetic, including policy-hook paths. Exported constants and retained values must preserve float64 kind.\n    94\t\n    95\t**MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:181](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:181) | The integer adapter misstates the refusal algebra | Preserve returned reasons separately from operational failures.**\n    96\t\n    97\tInteger v4 returns keyword reasons for bit limits, digit limits, and float overflow. Other refusals throw operational failures; they are not structured result maps for a generic guest handler. This is explicit in [yin.vm.integer:81](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:81).\n    98\t\n    99\tThe proposed signature omits a dedicated float-overflow policy and leaves `on-refusal` ambiguous. PHP’s `int-result` also performs int-range-to-float conversion rather than the same translation as Python/JS.\n   100\t\n   101\tDefine successful result shapes and all three returned reasons precisely. Do not catch and relabel wrong-type, arity, or invalid-kernel-operation defects as guest exceptions. Keep coercion and whitespace policy language-owned; a shared `skip-ws` needs an explicit character policy.\n   102\t\n   103\t**MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:196](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:196) | The profile schema does not yet establish the claimed complete, reproducible composition | Separate emission recipes from resolved runtime profiles.**\n   104\t\n   105\tThe illustrated profile pins component addresses but contains no address for the owning prelude’s code. Names, revision labels, and emitter options alone do not content-pin its definition vector.\n   106\t\n   107\tSimilarly, a list of component names/addresses is insufficient to validate transitive dependencies, exports, or cycles without the corresponding artifacts. Existing `prelude-admission` checks host availability and constructs specifications; it does not supply that artifact closure.\n   108\t\n   109\tSpecify an immutable, verified artifact set as validation input, pin the root runtime module, and reconcile component edges with dependency manifests. Check collisions by qualified bindings and explicit source projections; equal bare export names in distinct modules are legitimate.\n   110\t\n   111\tClarify whether `:effects` is an allowed-effect ceiling. If it merely repeats the footprint already derived by publication, omit the duplicate authority. A ceiling must be checked against the derived transitive footprint.\n   112\t\n   113\t**MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:459](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:459) | Migration promises and acceptance gates need reconciliation | Define address-neutral boundaries and phase-specific evidence.**\n   114\t\n   115\tP3 promises zero changes to existing languages, while D6 proposes making `yang.clojure` itself consume the new standalone library. Replacing current `library-for` bundling changes code and dependency identities. Keep existing defaults intact or schedule that migration as an explicit additional profile change.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
